{
  "abstract": "A complete Lean 4 / Mathlib proof of Ruzsa's 1990 theorem: there is an asymptotic additive basis A of order 2 of the natural numbers whose representation function is bounded in mean square, that is, every large n is a sum of two elements of A, and the number f(n) of ordered representations satisfies sum_{n <= x} f(n)^2 = O(x). This is the case r = 2 of Erdős Problem #1192, which asks the same question for every order r and is open for r >= 3. The statement is the formal-conjectures statement erdos_1192.variants.ruzsa, copied verbatim. The proof is not Ruzsa's. Three parabolas {(x,x^2)}, {(x,-x^2)} and {(x,(2v-1)x^2)} (v a quadratic non-residue) form a basis of order 2 of (Z/p)^2 with additive energy at most 333 p^2. This is lifted to Z/(2p)^2 by a base-2p digit gadget with carry bits, and then to N by placing one block at each of a sequence of geometrically growing scales (primes from Bertrand's postulate). The scales are glued together by a Minkowski inequality for r-fold additive energies, proved by Fourier analysis on Z/N. The development also proves, for every r, that Erdős #1192 at order r is equivalent to the existence of bases of every cyclic group Z/m with r-fold energy at most C m.",
